## Setting 2: Bezel Setting — Maximum Protection

A bezel setting wraps the asscher in a continuous band of metal around the entire girdle of the stone. The diamond is fully secured. All 8 corners are simultaneously covered.

The bezel is the safest setting choice from a stone-protection standpoint. There are no individual prongs to snag or wear down. The metal lip holds the stone from all sides.

The trade-off is face-up visibility. A bezel covers the girdle and a small amount of the crown edge. From directly above, the asscher looks slightly smaller than it would in a prong setting of the same carat weight.

**Square bezel vs. round bezel:** This matters for an asscher. A round bezel profile curves around the stone and fails the Geometry Test immediately. Look specifically for a square or straight-edged bezel that follows the asscher's octagonal shape without rounding the corners.

**Full bezel vs. half bezel:** A full bezel wraps the complete circumference. A half bezel leaves two sides partially open, covered only at the top and bottom. The half bezel exposes more stone while keeping some protection.

