## Setting 3: Cathedral Setting — Elevation and Structure

A cathedral setting raises the center stone above the band on two arched metal supports, like the flying buttresses of a gothic cathedral. The stone sits high. The band is visible below the stone on both sides of the ring.

The elevated position creates two practical benefits. First, the stone is above most surfaces when worn, which reduces daily contact risk. Second, more light reaches the stone from the sides, which amplifies the Hall of Mirrors reflections in the asscher's step-cut facets.

The visual character of a cathedral setting is structural and formal. The arches add height and presence. On an asscher center, this creates a particularly striking look — the stone's square geometry sits elevated and dominant over the band.

**The geometry concern:** Many cathedral settings incorporate curved arch profiles with scrollwork or floral detailing at the shoulders. These curved details fail the Geometry Test when paired with an asscher. Look for cathedral settings with straight or angular arch profiles. A straight-sided cathedral with no decorative curves passes the Geometry Test cleanly.

**Cathedral vs. prong:** The difference is mostly visual presence. A prong solitaire sits closer to the finger. A cathedral setting elevates the ring's profile and adds visual height. Neither is functionally superior — the choice is style and how much profile you want the ring to carry.

