## Setting 5: Vintage and Art Deco Settings

The asscher cut was born in the Art Deco period. It was designed in 1902 and became the defining center stone of 1920s fine jewelry. Vintage settings and Art Deco settings are not just compatible with an asscher — they are historically correct.

The elements that define Art Deco and vintage asscher settings: milgrain borders, geometric filigree, engraved angular patterns, and side stone arrangements using baguettes or single-cut rounds in channel settings. Every one of these elements passes the Geometry Test because Art Deco design is itself geometric and angular.

Farzana describes this as the most naturally aligned pairing in the asscher category: the stone and the setting speak exactly the same visual language. The step-cut's parallel facets echo the geometric patterns in Art Deco metalwork.

**What to look for in a vintage setting:**

- Milgrain border along the basket and band edges — the tiny bead texture is the single most reliable marker of authentic vintage styling
- Geometric engraving or filigree (angular, not floral)
- Platinum or white gold metal — yellow gold vintage settings exist but are less traditional for asscher
- Side stone channels with baguette or single-cut stones, not modern round brilliants

