## Expert Summary

The right asscher setting is not a style preference. It is a functional decision based on the stone's geometry, its corner vulnerability, and the visual character of its step-cut facets.

For most buyers, a 4-prong V-tip solitaire is the correct starting point. It is the most honest setting for an asscher — it gets out of the way and lets the Hall of Mirrors effect do its work. From there, every other setting type adds something: a halo adds visual size, a vintage setting adds historical context, a bezel adds protection, a three-stone adds structured presence.

Every addition comes with a trade-off. A halo adds cost. A bezel slightly reduces the face-up view.

A three-stone requires careful side stone selection. None of these trade-offs are dealbreakers — they are decisions based on what the buyer values most.

> **Farzana's take:** The most common mistake I see is buyers choosing the setting before choosing the stone. The stone's proportions, its exact millimeter size, its prong requirements — all of that needs to drive the setting decision. A 5.5mm asscher needs a different basket than a 6.5mm asscher. Get the stone first. Then fit the setting to it, not the other way around.

